<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  ecr88_params.xacro
  Volvo ECR88 short-swing excavator + Xpanner X1 PanelLift front end
  Parameter set for URDF -> USD asset-ization (Isaac Sim 4.x / 5.x,
  isaacsim.asset.importer.urdf).

  This file contains PARAMETERS ONLY. No links, no joints, no macros.
  Include it first from ecr88.urdf.xacro.

  ============================================================================
  PROVENANCE
  ============================================================================
  PRIMARY (authoritative, every dist*/len*/ang* below):
    resources/ECR88_kinematic_parameters.md
      Sheet `KinematicPara_new`, column "ECR88 기장"
      (origin: SharePoint  task-specific-automation / Shared Documents /
       X1 - Excavator / DetailConecpt / Kinematic / 260801 /
       Kinematic Measurement parameter_MachineModel_ProductionV1.xlsx)

  SECONDARY (reference only, used ONLY where KinematicPara_new has no row:
  the boundary boxes, the PanelLift/fork geometry and the suction plate):
    Same file, sheet `old` [hidden] — raw Leica MC1 + Total Station readings.

  Property names are byte-identical to the spreadsheet parameter names
  (including the source's own inconsistencies, e.g. LenUppX1 vs LenUpX) so
  this file diffs 1:1 against the sheet. Do not "tidy" the names.

  Units: length [m], angle [rad] (written in the sheet's own `pi/180*deg` form
  inside a xacro expression).

  ============================================================================
  NAMING: SHEET VOCABULARY vs URDF CONTRACT VOCABULARY
  ============================================================================
  Sections [4]-[14] keep the SPREADSHEET names byte-identical, so this file
  diffs 1:1 against KinematicPara_new (see the note above).
  ecr88.urdf.xacro consumes a DIFFERENT vocabulary — the "REQUIRED PROPERTY
  CONTRACT" block at the top of that file: per-axis dist*_x/_y/_z, lowercase
  lenBottom*/lenUpp*, and the m_* / geo_* / lim_* / eff_* / vel_* / dyn_* /
  bkt_mimic_* families.
  Section [15] at the bottom of this file is the single place where the two
  meet: every contract name is defined there, either as an alias onto the
  sheet-named property above it (no new number) or as a clearly labelled
  ESTIMATE. Do not define a contract name anywhere else and do not let an
  alias drift into a second, independent number.

  ============================================================================
  ROWS DELIBERATELY NOT DEFINED  (처리구분 = "Delete" in KinematicPara_new)
  ============================================================================
    distUcToFork, distForkToPanelBottom, distUcToForkUpRef,
    distUcToForkUpPnt1, distUcToForkDownPnt2, distUcToForkDownPnt3,
    angAttToContactSurface
  They must NOT enter the URDF. If you find yourself needing one, the chain
  is wrong — go back to the sheet.

  ============================================================================
  !!! TODO — VALUES THAT ARE **NOT** IN ANY SOURCE WE HOLD !!!
  ============================================================================
  Everything in section [13] and [14] is invented. Nothing below is measured.
  Summary of every guess in this file, in one place:

   T1  ALL joint limits (lower/upper) — section [13].
       Source needed: cylinder stroke (retracted/extended) + the pin geometry
       already in section [5]/[6], OR the calibration videos at
       SharePoint .../Kinematic/260801/CalibrationVideo/ (7 clips,
       Chassis/Boom/Arm/Bucket/Travel/Tilt/Rotator, 3.03 GB).
       The Length-Kinematic deck contains ZERO joint ranges — confirmed by
       full 13-page extraction.
       WORST OF ALL: jntBkt* (bucket_joint, the output link about the bucket
       pin) and jntConnRod* (conn_rod_joint, the cut member of the 4-bar) have
       no source AND no near-miss row to rename — the sheet has no bucket or
       conn-rod angle of any kind. They are pure invention, see the per-block
       assumptions in section [13].
   T2  ALL effort limits — section [13]. Source needed: hydraulic cylinder
       bore/rod diameter + relief pressure from the ECR88 service manual,
       or Volvo's published digging/tearout forces.
   T3  ALL velocity limits — section [13]. Source needed: Volvo ECR88D spec
       sheet (swing speed, cycle times) or measured telemetry.
   T4  ALL masses — section [14]. Source needed: ECR88 3D model (Olivia) —
       per-part volume x material density — or Volvo part weights.
       BOTH the per-link split AND the total are guesses; the split is not
       normalised to any total. See T10 and the method note in section [14].
       The m_* family (section [15], one entry per link the URDF actually
       emits) is AUTHORITATIVE; the older mass_* family in section [14] is
       kept only as an alias onto it so the two can never disagree again.
   T5  ALL inertia tensors — section [14]. Currently computed by the
       bounding-box solid approximation in ecr88_inertia.xacro from the
       estimated masses + estimated box dims. Replace with real tensors from
       the CAD model. Slide-2 row 30 of the calibration deck lists
       "CalibInertialTensor ... (TBD)" — upstream has not measured it either.
       (2026-09-14: the firmware has no step 30 and no CalibInertialTensor at all;
       the nearest thing, CalibSwingStopCoeff = 32, is unreachable - no state in
       AutoStsMgr/Chart, and its calculation is dead-code-eliminated. The conclusion
       stands: nobody upstream has identified inertia.)
   T6  Boundary box HEIGHTS (LenBottomZ_est, LenUppZ_est) — section [9].
       The sheet gives box ORIGINS and X/Y sizes but no Z size. Derived, see
       inline assumption.
   T7  Joint axis directions (<axis xyz>) are NOT in any source — the X/Y/Z
       arrows exist only as figure labels on non-extractable slides. They are
       asserted in ecr88_macros.xacro, not here, but they are equally a guess.
   T8  Bkt IMU mount offset — the deck offers "On Link / On Bkt" as a config
       choice and gives no offset for either. See section [3].
   T9  Suction plate footprint LenSDX / LenSDY are literally 0 in the source
       for ECR88 (DX650 has 0.7 x 2.0). Treated as "not measured", NOT as a
       zero-size plate. See section [11].
  T10  mass_total_reference (8500 kg, section [14]) — a RECALLED Volvo ECR88(D)
       brochure "operating weight", NOT read out of any document we hold.
       `grep -rn '8\.5|8500|weight|중량' resources/` finds no operating-weight
       row in any of the three source extracts. It is an ESTIMATE like every
       other number in [14], it is NOT a datum the split is normalised to, and
       it MUST be confirmed against the real ECR88(D) spec sheet (or simply
       deleted) before anyone cites a machine weight from this asset.
  T11  Primitive placeholder geometry geo_* — section [15][D]. The collision /
       visual primitives need cross-section dimensions (boom radius, track
       width, pad thickness, ...) that no source we hold contains. Two of them
       are DERIVATIONS from measured numbers (geo_house_h from the T6 height
       derivation, geo_pad_x/geo_pad_y from the T9 stand-in); the rest are
       invented. They vanish the moment real meshes land (use_meshes = true),
       but until then they also feed the inertia tensors (T5), so a wrong
       radius is a wrong tensor.

  ============================================================================
  KNOWN SOURCE CONFLICTS (resolved in favour of KinematicPara_new, logged here)
  ============================================================================
   C1  `old` sheet vs KinematicPara_new rounding:
         LenBm  3.551 vs lenBm1 3.55 | LenAm 1.699 vs lenArm 1.7
         LenBktInp 0.423 vs lenInpLink 0.42
         LenBktConn 0.402 vs lenConnRod 0.4
         LenBktGrnd 0.259 vs lenGndLink 0.2597
       -> KinematicPara_new is the release value. Used.
   C2  Length-Kinematic deck vs KinematicPara_new on the GNSS frames:
         deck distAntMainToChs (0.575,-0.08,-1.398) vs sheet (0.57,-0.087,-1.416)
         deck distAntMainToAntAux (0.678,0.958,0.001) vs sheet (0.682,0.959,0)
       -> sheet used. The deck shows the MC1->X1 derivation; the sheet shows
          the value actually shipped. Do not "fix" the sheet with deck maths.
   C3  The deck asserts "lenToCylSmlBkt is the same value as lenGndLink".
       In the release sheet lenToCylSmlBkt = 0.42 and lenGndLink = 0.2597,
       so that is false as written. Note that lenToCylSmlBkt (0.42) ==
       lenInpLink (0.42) exactly, so the deck note is most likely a typo for
       lenInpLink. NOT asserted — flagged. Verify before trusting the 4-bar.
   C4  `old` sheet row 31 maps Len1BktX = 0.256 -> lenToCylSmlBkt, but
       KinematicPara_new says lenToCylSmlBkt = 0.42. Unresolved. Same warning
       as C3: the bucket linkage is the least trustworthy part of this file.
   C5  distTiltMntToTilt: `old` gives LenTRX 0.221 / LenTRZ -0.442,
       KinematicPara_new gives (0.28, 0, -0.22) and distRotToAtt Z -0.2615.
       Note -0.22 + -0.2615 = -0.4815 ~ LenTRZ -0.442 but NOT equal. The
       release sheet re-split the TR chain across two frames. Used as-is.
   C6  angTiltMntToTilt: `old` AlphaTR1 = 0.0339 rad (1.94 deg),
       KinematicPara_new = pi/180*1.736. Release value used.

  ============================================================================
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="ecr88_params">

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [1] BUILD FLAGS
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- Meshes do not exist yet (Olivia holds the ECR88 3D model). While false,
       every link renders/collides as a primitive derived from the boundary
       numbers in section [9] and the link lengths in section [5].
       When the meshes land: flip this to true, drop the files into
       assets/ecr88/meshes/{visual,collision}/ and nothing else changes. -->
  <xacro:property name="use_meshes"            value="false"/>
  <xacro:property name="mesh_dir"              value="package://xpanner_sim/assets/ecr88/meshes"/>
  <xacro:property name="mesh_ext"              value=".obj"/>

  <!-- Collision primitives are capsules/boxes sized from len* when true.
       Isaac's URDF importer is happier with convex primitives than with
       concave meshes, so keep this true even after use_meshes flips. -->
  <xacro:property name="collision_from_primitives" value="true"/>

  <!-- Emit the Probe / ContactSurface / Ant* frames as zero-mass fixed links.
       ContactSurface is the TCP we publish to ROS 2. -->
  <xacro:property name="publish_tool_frames"   value="true"/>
  <xacro:property name="tcp_frame"             value="contact_surface"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [2] MACHINE VARIANT SELECTOR
       See section [12] for the values each variant switches.
       ======================================================================== -->
  <!-- One of:
         ECR88_KIJANG              = KinematicPara_new col "ECR88 기장"
         ECR88_US1_2P1M            = KinematicPara_new col "ECR88 2.1m 미국#1"  (THIS BUILD)
         ECR88_US1_2P1M_NEWSUCTION = KinematicPara_new col "... 신규흡착기"

       2026-09-11: switched KIJANG (arm 1.7 m) -> US1_2P1M (arm 2.1 m).
       Teo (Slack #simulation, 11:34 KST): "Arm의 경우 저희가 주로 사용하는 사양은
       2.1m로 보시면 될 것 같아요", and the Volvo dimension sheet he attached
       highlights the 2.1 m dipper row. 2.1 m is the fleet spec; 1.7 m was only ever
       the default because "기장" is the first column of the parameter sheet.

       This moves more than lenArm. It also moves distArmToInpLinkX, lenToCylSmlArm2,
       lenToCylLrgBkt, lenToCylLrgBkt2, angCylLrgBkt (see the branch in section [12]),
       the arm assembly MASS (280 -> 340 kg published, see ecr88_dynamics.xacro), and
       it invalidates the boom ground-contact anchor, which was solved with lenArm=1.7
       and has been re-solved for 2.1 m. -->
  <!-- Selectable from the command line since 2026-09-14:
         xacro ecr88.urdf.xacro machine_variant:=ECR88_KIJANG
       Reason: the X1Exc generated C in the repo is compiled with ECR88D_ShortArm.m
       (lenArm 1.7), so a firmware-in-the-loop run against THAT binary needs the 1.7 m
       geometry. The default stays the 2.1 m fleet build. -->
  <xacro:arg name="machine_variant" default="ECR88_US1_2P1M"/>
  <xacro:property name="machine_variant"       value="$(arg machine_variant)"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [3] MACHINE CONFIGURATION OPTIONS
       Source: Length Kinematic Measurement_ProductionV1.pptx slide 2
       ("Machine Model" screen). These are Enable/Disable switches on the HMI,
       reproduced here so the URDF matches the tablet configuration.
       ======================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="cfg_machine_model"     value="ECR88"/>
  <xacro:property name="cfg_boom"              value="3.5m_standard"/>
  <!-- lenBm2 = 0 in this build => the second boom segment is absent.
       Keep the link as a zero-length fixed joint so the chain indices stay
       stable across variants; do not delete it from the tree. -->
  <xacro:property name="cfg_dual_boom_enable"  value="false"/>
  <!-- BoomSwing sensor mounted on BmMnt. distBmMntToBm1 is non-zero ONLY when
       this is true; it is (0,0,0) here. -->
  <xacro:property name="cfg_boomswing_sensor_on_bmmnt" value="false"/>
  <xacro:property name="cfg_tilt_coupler_enable"       value="false"/>
  <xacro:property name="cfg_tilt_rotator_model"        value="JK"/>
  <!-- T8: "On Link" or "On Bkt". The deck gives NO mounting offset for either
       option, so the IMU frame is currently coincident with its parent. -->
  <xacro:property name="cfg_bkt_imu_mount"     value="On Link"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [4] FRAME OFFSETS  (dist*)  — KinematicPara_new, ECR88 기장
       These become <joint><origin xyz="..."/> entries, parent->child.
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- chassis (house) -> boom mount.
       Y is negative: MC1 measures "Roll Length" +0.159 and X1 PanelLift
       applies *(-1). X (= MC1 "Pitch Length") is NOT negated. -->
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMntX"       value="0.94"/>
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMntY"       value="-0.15"/>
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMntZ"       value="0.0"/>

  <!-- boom mount -> boom1. Exists as a parameter purely to carry the
       BoomSwing sensor option (cfg_boomswing_sensor_on_bmmnt). Zero here. -->
  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1X"       value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1Y"       value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1Z"       value="0.0"/>

  <!-- arm -> input link (start of the bucket 4-bar).
       NOTE the non-zero Z: the input-link pin is NOT coaxial with the arm
       plane. A planar-linkage assumption is wrong by 18.5 mm.
       X is variant-dependent (1.441 / 1.841) -> section [12]. -->
  <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLinkY"     value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLinkZ"     value="0.0185"/>

  <!-- output link -> tilt mount. Coincident frames, kept for chain clarity. -->
  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMntX" value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMntY" value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMntZ" value="0.0"/>

  <!-- tilt mount -> tilt. The deck labels the tiltrotator "Tilt = Rot",
       i.e. tilt and rotate axes intersect, so there is NO tilt->rot offset
       parameter at all. The rot joint origin is (0,0,0) by construction. -->
  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTiltX"    value="0.28"/>
  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTiltY"    value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTiltZ"    value="-0.22"/>

  <!-- rotator -> attachment (Att. = Attachment Centre Of Rotation) -->
  <xacro:property name="distRotToAttX"         value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distRotToAttY"         value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distRotToAttZ"         value="-0.2615"/>

  <!-- attachment -> probe -> contact surface (panel-lift suction tool).
       The sheet is internally consistent here:
         distAttToContactSurface = distAttToProbe + distProbeToContactSurface
         X: 0.35 + 0.1   = 0.45
         Z: -0.572 + -0.264 = -0.836
       distAttToContactSurface is kept as its own property (it is a sheet row
       and the HMI displays it) but the macros build the chain additively, so
       the two agree by construction. All three are variant-dependent under
       the new-suction variant -> section [12]. -->
  <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeY"       value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceY" value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceY"   value="0.0"/>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Cylinder pin attachment points (dist*Cyl*).
       Needed ONLY if cylinders are modelled as separate links or a
       mimic/closed-chain solver is used. They are static mounting-pin
       offsets, NOT strokes — there are no strokes in any source (T1).
       WARNING: the reference pose these are measured from is slide 10 of the
       Length-Kinematic deck, which is 100% pictorial and produced no text.
       Do not trust the cylinder chain until that slide is opened in the
       original binary.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1X"        value="0.272"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1Y"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1Z"        value="0.391"/>

  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1X"        value="1.715"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1Y"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1Z"        value="0.58"/>

  <!-- Bm2 option inactive in this build (cfg_dual_boom_enable = false). -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm2X"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm2Y"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm2Z"        value="0.0"/>

  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm2X"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm2Y"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm2Z"        value="0.0"/>

  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArmX"        value="-1.508"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArmY"        value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArmZ"        value="1.08"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [5] LINK LENGTHS  (len*)  — KinematicPara_new, ECR88 기장
       ======================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBm1"                value="3.55"/>
  <!-- lenBm2 = 0 => dual-boom segment absent. Emitted as a zero-length fixed
       joint, not deleted, so link indices are variant-stable. -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBm2"                value="0.0"/>
  <!-- lenArm is variant-dependent (1.7 / 2.1) -> section [12]. -->

  <!-- Bucket 4-bar: input link -> connecting rod -> output link, closed by
       the ground link on the arm. See C3/C4 before trusting this. -->
  <xacro:property name="lenInpLink"            value="0.42"/>
  <xacro:property name="lenConnRod"            value="0.4"/>
  <xacro:property name="lenOutpLink"           value="0.33"/>
  <xacro:property name="lenGndLink"            value="0.2597"/>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Cylinder-side lengths. 처리구분 "Calculated by Mango" means these are
       derived by the controller from the pin offsets above, not measured
       independently. Kept so the URDF and the controller agree numerically.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBm1"        value="0.476"/>   <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlBm1"        value="1.81"/>    <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBm2"        value="0.0"/>     <!-- Calculated by Mango, Bm2 inactive -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlBm2"        value="0.0"/>     <!-- Calculated by Mango, Bm2 inactive -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgArm"        value="1.855"/>   <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlArm"        value="0.53"/>
  <!-- lenToCylSmlArm2, lenToCylLrgBkt, lenToCylLrgBkt2 are variant-dependent
       -> section [12]. -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt1"       value="0.394"/>
  <!-- C3/C4: deck says this equals lenGndLink (0.2597) — it does not.
       It equals lenInpLink (0.42) exactly. Flagged, not resolved. -->
  <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlBkt"        value="0.42"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [6] ANGLES  (ang*)  — KinematicPara_new, ECR88 기장
       Written in the sheet's own `pi/180*deg` form inside a xacro expression.
       ALL of these are fixed MOUNTING offsets. NONE of them is a joint limit
       (the only exception is angForkUpLimit, section [10]).
       ======================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="angArmToGndLink"       value="${pi/180*4.1}"/>      <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <xacro:property name="angOutpLinkToTiltMnt"  value="0.0"/>
  <!-- +1.736 deg. A genuine non-zero rpy term on the tilt-mount joint. -->
  <xacro:property name="angTiltMntToTilt"      value="${pi/180*1.736}"/>
  <xacro:property name="angRotToAtt"           value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="angAttToProbe"         value="0.0"/>                <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <xacro:property name="angProbeToContactSurface" value="0.0"/>             <!-- Calculated by Mango -->
  <!-- angAttToContactSurface: 처리구분 = Delete. NOT DEFINED. -->

  <!-- Cylinder mounting angles ("Calculated by Mango"). Same slide-10
       reference-pose caveat as the cylinder offsets in section [4]. -->
  <xacro:property name="angCylLrgBm1"          value="${pi/180*55.2}"/>
  <xacro:property name="angCylSmlBm1"          value="${-pi/180*18.7}"/>
  <xacro:property name="angCylLrgBm2"          value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="angCylSmlBm2"          value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="angCylLrgArm"          value="${-pi/180*144.4}"/>
  <xacro:property name="angCylSmlArm"          value="${-pi/180*150}"/>
  <!-- angCylLrgBkt is variant-dependent (-165.8 / -164.1 deg) -> section [12]. -->

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [7] GNSS ANTENNA POSITIONS
       Source: KinematicPara_new (release values).
       Raw Leica MC1 readings, for traceability only, NOT used:
         Left  GPS (= AntMain)  X  0.103   Y  1.038   Z  1.346
         Right GPS (= AntAux)   X -0.575   Y  0.080   Z  1.345
       MC1 -> X1 PanelLift applies *(-1) on all three distAntMainToChs axes,
       plus a +0.053 m bolt-height correction on Z (deck call-outs 50mm+3mm).
       distAntMainToAntAux is a pure difference of the two GPS readings with
       NO sign flip.
       See C2: the deck's arithmetic lands 5-18 mm away from these release
       values. The release values win.
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- AntMain -> Chassis. Invert this vector to place the antenna link on the
       house: p_ant_in_chs = -distAntMainToChs. -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChsX"     value="0.57"/>
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChsY"     value="-0.087"/>
  <!-- CONFLICT, resolved in favour of the firmware. The release sheet says -1.416 for
       ALL THREE variants; ECR88D_LongArm.m and ECR88D_ShortArm.m both say -1.345, a
       71 mm difference. X and Y agree exactly in every source, so this is a pure stack
       height change: same antenna footprint, different mount or spacer. The sheet row is
       marked "Display on Tablet", i.e. it feeds an operator readout, which is the kind of
       row that does not get revised when hardware moves. The firmware value is what the
       machine's position solution is actually computed with, so for SIL it is the one
       that has to be here.
       WORTH RAISING WITH OLIVIA. An earlier revision argued "71 mm is larger than the
       firmware's own placement tolerance (releaseHgt = 20 mm)". That yardstick is void:
       the Tol_t struct holding releaseHgt has ZERO references in the generated
       MdlApp.c - it is declared, never enforced. The difference matters anyway, and
       more directly: links.chs.p = mainAntenna + R_chs * distAntMainToChs is the ONLY
       offset joining the GNSS fix to the kinematic chain, so 71 mm lands in every tool
       position the firmware computes. The gates that ARE enforced are 1.0 deg per joint
       and 0.20 m lateral on the undercarriage (resources/X1Exc_SIL_spec.md A6).
       Note LenUpZ_est below is derived from this, so the cab roof estimate moves with it. -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChsZ"     value="-1.345"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  (sheet: -1.416) -->

  <!-- AntMain -> AntAux. Dual-antenna heading baseline: |(0.682,0.959,0)|
       = 1.1797 m. Expressed relative to AntMain, NOT to the chassis. -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAuxX"  value="0.682"/>
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAuxY"  value="0.959"/>
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAuxZ"  value="0.0"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [8] (reserved)
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [9] BOUNDARY BOX  (LenBottom* / LenUpp*)
       Source: sheet `old` [hidden], ECR88 column. There is NO equivalent row
       in KinematicPara_new, so this is the only geometry source we have and
       it is used knowingly.
       Purpose: primitive collision boxes for the undercarriage and the upper
       structure while meshes are missing (use_meshes = false).
       *X1/*Y1/*Z1 are the box ORIGIN (min corner) in the chassis frame;
       *X/*Y are the box SIZE. Names kept verbatim including the source's own
       LenUppX1-vs-LenUpX inconsistency.
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- Undercarriage / lower boundary -->
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomX1"           value="-0.44"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomY1"           value="-1.08"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomZ1"           value="-1.445"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomX"            value="2.82"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomY"            value="2.32"/>

  <!-- Upper structure (house) / upper boundary -->
  <xacro:property name="LenUppX1"              value="-0.41"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenUppY1"              value="-1.01"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenUppZ1"              value="-0.67"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenUpX"                value="2.28"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenUpY"                value="2.46"/>

  <!-- T6 ESTIMATE. The sheet has no LenBottomZ (height). Assumption: the top
       of the undercarriage is the bottom of the upper structure, i.e.
       LenUppZ1 - LenBottomZ1 = -0.67 - (-1.445) = 0.775.
       This is a DERIVATION from two measured numbers, not a measurement.
       (Declared after LenUppZ1 so the file does not depend on xacro's lazy
       property evaluation.) -->
  <xacro:property name="LenBottomZ_est"        value="${LenUppZ1 - LenBottomZ1}"/>
  <!-- T6 ESTIMATE. The sheet has no LenUpZ (height). Assumption: the cab roof
       sits one bolt-height (0.053, the deck's own antenna correction) below
       the antenna mounting plane, and the antenna plane is at
       -distAntMainToChsZ = +1.416 in the chassis frame. So
         height = (1.416 - 0.053) - (-0.67) = 2.033.
       Pure inference. Replace from the CAD model. -->
  <xacro:property name="LenUpZ_est"            value="${(-distAntMainToChsZ - 0.053) - LenUppZ1}"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [10] PANELLIFT / FORK
       Sources are mixed — read the per-row comment:
         dist*/ang* rows  -> KinematicPara_new, 처리구분 "Update after
                             Calibration". These MUST stay xacro parameters;
                             the calibration procedure overwrites them.
         LenPL* / AlphaPL* -> sheet `old` [hidden], ECR88 column. Reference
                             measurements, no release row exists.
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- 처리구분 = Update after Calibration. Do NOT hardcode downstream.
       2026-09-14: the sheet's 기장 values (1.988, 0, -0.66) are the PRE-calibration
       entry; the calibrated value the firmware actually runs is in the compiled X1Exc
       binary (ECR88D_ShortArm.m:176, MdlApp.c:499): (1.844, 0, -0.7022). Adopted.
       ForkBack is the pose CalibForkRefPose (step 28) records with the contact surface
       held against the backrest, so its origin is where the cups meet the stack, not
       the corner of the pallet. The uc frame is the chassis origin (MdlApp.c:12754,
       zero offset). ECR88D_LongArm.m is on the OLD schema and has no distUcToForkBack,
       so the 2.1 m build borrows the 1.7 m unit's calibrated carriage: same
       undercarriage and fork hardware assumed. -->
  <xacro:property name="distUcToForkBackX"     value="1.844"/>  <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE (ShortArm unit; sheet pre-calibration 1.988) -->
  <xacro:property name="distUcToForkBackY"     value="0.0"/>    <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE -->
  <xacro:property name="distUcToForkBackZ"     value="-0.7022"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE (sheet pre-calibration -0.66) -->

  <!-- 처리구분 = Update after Calibration. -->
  <xacro:property name="distForkBackToPanelTopX" value="1.0165"/>
  <xacro:property name="distForkBackToPanelTopY" value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="distForkBackToPanelTopZ" value="0.0"/>
  <!-- 1.0165 matches the compiled firmware exactly (ECR88D_ShortArm.m:177). It is measured
       ALONG THE FORK BLADE: panelTop = forkBack * Ry(pi/2) (+) this offset in the forkBack
       frame (MdlApp.c:12788). The modules stand on the tilted pallet leaning on the
       backrest; "top" is the outermost module face, 1.0165 m out along the blade, and the
       tool meets it face-on, approaching back toward the machine. 30 x 30 mm = 0.90 m of
       glass fills that blade. -->
  <xacro:property name="TarPanelPickupMargin"  value="0.5"/>    <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  SysPar.m, m. Approach point = panelTop (+) [0,0,margin]: 0.5 m out in front of the outer face (MdlApp.c:12930). -->

  <!-- 처리구분 = Update after Calibration.
       The ONE row in the whole parameter set that is genuinely a joint LIMIT
       rather than a mounting offset. The sheet gives it to the nearest degree; the
       firmware carries the calibrated value, and "Update after Calibration" is the
       sheet telling us to expect exactly that. VARIANT SPECIFIC:
         ECR88D_LongArm.m  (US, 2.1 m)  -14.19471 deg   <- this build
         ECR88D_ShortArm.m (KR, 1.7 m)  -16.3246  deg
       so the sheet's -16 was the Korean machine's number all along. -->
  <xacro:property name="angForkUpLimit"        value="${-pi/180*14.19471}"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE -->

  <!-- Fork / panel geometry, sheet `old`, reference only -->
  <xacro:property name="LenPLX"                value="1.086"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLY"                value="-0.385"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLZ"                value="-0.915"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLWidth"            value="1.2"/>     <!-- panel width -->
  <!-- Source note: "현장 패널 높이(시험 시 포크 높이로 임의)" — the field panel
       height was set arbitrarily to the fork height during the test. So this
       is a TEST value, not a product dimension. -->
  <xacro:property name="LenPanelHeight"        value="1.085"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPL5Width"           value="0.405"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLX2"               value="0.3"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLZ2"               value="1.08"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLX5"               value="1.015"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLZ5"               value="0.287"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLX7"               value="0.13"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenPLZ7"               value="0.475"/>
  <!-- Already in radians in the source (protractor readings 74.5 / 15.7 deg).
       NOT re-converted — 1.3002 rad = 74.50 deg, 0.274 rad = 15.70 deg, both
       check out against the source's own note. -->
  <xacro:property name="AlphaPL1"              value="1.3002"/>  <!-- fork upper support bar tilt -->
  <xacro:property name="AlphaPL2"              value="0.274"/>   <!-- fork lower pallet tilt -->

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [11] SUCTION PLATE
       Source: sheet `old` [hidden], ECR88 column.
       ======================================================================== -->
  <!-- T9: the ECR88 column records 0 for both plate dimensions (DX650 records
       0.7 x 2.0). A zero-area suction plate is physically meaningless, so
       these are treated as NOT MEASURED, and the *_est values below are used
       for the visual/collision box. Get the real footprint from the X1
       PanelLift tool drawing. -->
  <xacro:property name="LenSDX"                value="0.0"/>     <!-- suction plate width  — 0 = not measured -->
  <xacro:property name="LenSDY"                value="0.0"/>     <!-- suction plate height — 0 = not measured -->
  <!-- This one IS real: adapter height + adapter->plate-bottom height. -->
  <xacro:property name="LenSDZ"                value="-0.84"/>
  <!-- ESTIMATE, used only when LenSDX/LenSDY are 0. Assumption: the plate is
       sized to the panel it lifts, so it is LenPLWidth (1.2) wide and
       LenPanelHeight (1.085) tall. Purely a stand-in for rendering. -->
  <xacro:property name="LenSDX_est"            value="${LenPLWidth}"/>
  <xacro:property name="LenSDY_est"            value="${LenPanelHeight}"/>
  <!-- Sanity cross-check (not used, kept for the reviewer): the suction stack
       LenSDZ = -0.84 sits within 4 mm of distAttToContactSurfaceZ = -0.836.
       Two independent measurements of the same physical distance. Good sign. -->

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [12] VARIANT-DEPENDENT VALUES
       Defined exactly once, in whichever branch matches machine_variant, with
       scope="global" so they are visible to the rest of the document. No
       property is redefined, so xacro emits no redefinition warnings.

       KinematicPara_new columns, side by side:
         parameter           기장      2.1m 美#1   2.1m 美#1 신규흡착기
         lenArm              1.7       2.1        2.1
         distArmToInpLink X  1.441     1.841      1.841
         lenToCylSmlArm2     2.175     2.572      2.572
         lenToCylLrgBkt      1.525     1.532      1.532
         lenToCylLrgBkt2     1.441     1.841      1.841
         angCylLrgBkt       -165.8deg -164.1deg  -164.1deg
         distAttToProbe      0.35/-0.572          0.255/-0.57
         distProbeToContact  0.1/-0.264           0.1/-0.262
         distAttToContact    0.45/-0.836          0.355/-0.832
         distUcToForkBack    1.988/-0.66  (zeros) 1.523/0
         distForkBackToPanelTop 1.0165    (zeros) 2.608/0
       (the 美#1 column zeroes the fork rows; that column's fork geometry has
        not been measured, so the fork chain is only valid for 기장 and
        신규흡착기 — see the guard comment in the US1 branch.)
       ======================================================================== -->

  <xacro:if value="${machine_variant == 'ECR88_KIJANG'}">
    <xacro:property name="lenArm"              value="1.7"     scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLinkX"   value="1.441"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlArm2"     value="2.175"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt"      value="1.525"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt2"     value="1.441"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="angCylLrgBkt"        value="${-pi/180*165.8}" scope="global"/>
    <!-- 2026-09-15: suction head offsets are the FIRMWARE's for this unit. ECR88D_ShortArm.m:173-174
         (the 1.7 m unit the repo's binary is compiled with) carries (0.255, 0, -0.57) and
         (0.1, 0, -0.262) - the sheet's 신규흡착기 values - while the sheet's 기장 column still has
         the older head (0.35/-0.572, 0.1/-0.264). OEM-FIRMWARE beats the sheet. Found by the
         Isaac step-28 SIL run: the tool sat 92 mm off the firmware's contact surface. -->
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeX"     value="0.255"   scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ShortArm -->
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeZ"     value="-0.57"   scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ShortArm -->
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceX" value="0.1"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceZ" value="-0.262" scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ShortArm -->
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceX"   value="0.355"  scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceZ"   value="-0.832" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="fork_chain_valid"    value="true"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="angForkUpLimit"      value="${-pi/180*16.3246}" scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ECR88D_ShortArm.m:210 -->
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnX" value="-3.0" scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ECR88D_ShortArm.m:178 -->
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnZ" value="2.0"  scope="global"/>
    <!-- IMU mounting rotations = rpy(M transpose) of the ECR88D_ShortArm.m (1.7 m, the unit the repo's binary is compiled for) imu* matrices. Mounts are PER-UNIT calibration
         results: pairing a URDF from one unit with a firmware carrying another misreads arm/link by ~4 deg
         with no fault (sil/tests/test_imu_kinematics.py). -->
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_chs" value="-0.001755416 -0.002555442 -1.783640195" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_boom" value="-1.567613598 -0.571866959 0.006005104" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_arm" value="-1.573310335 0.033289461 -0.003775542" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_link" value="-1.575791294 0.068579979 -0.006844645" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_tilt" value="1.579079426 -0.015406954 -1.622796371" scope="global"/>
  </xacro:if>

  <xacro:if value="${machine_variant == 'ECR88_US1_2P1M'}">
    <xacro:property name="lenArm"              value="2.1"     scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLinkX"   value="1.841"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlArm2"     value="2.572"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt"      value="1.532"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt2"     value="1.841"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="angCylLrgBkt"        value="${-pi/180*164.1}" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeX"     value="0.35"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeZ"     value="-0.572"  scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceX" value="0.1"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceZ" value="-0.264" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceX"   value="0.45"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceZ"   value="-0.836" scope="global"/>
    <!-- The 美#1 column zeroes distUcToForkBack and distForkBackToPanelTop.
         Zero is "not measured", not "coincident", so the fork chain must be
         suppressed for this variant rather than built at the origin. -->
    <xacro:property name="fork_chain_valid"    value="false"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnX" value="-1.0" scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ECR88D_LongArm.m:170 -->
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnZ" value="2.0"  scope="global"/>
    <!-- IMU mounting rotations = rpy(M transpose) of the ECR88D_LongArm.m (2.1 m US unit) imu* matrices. Mounts are PER-UNIT calibration
         results: pairing a URDF from one unit with a firmware carrying another misreads arm/link by ~4 deg
         with no fault (sil/tests/test_imu_kinematics.py). -->
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_chs" value="-0.004742750 -0.008549333 -1.762994439" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_boom" value="-1.571816342 -0.566316481 -0.000590754" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_arm" value="-1.579068528 0.105440054 -0.003310372" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_link" value="-1.575935070 0.058856870 0.001290998" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_tilt" value="1.549803632 -0.010023128 -1.597706287" scope="global"/>
  </xacro:if>

  <xacro:if value="${machine_variant == 'ECR88_US1_2P1M_NEWSUCTION'}">
    <xacro:property name="lenArm"              value="2.1"     scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLinkX"   value="1.841"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylSmlArm2"     value="2.572"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt"      value="1.532"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="lenToCylLrgBkt2"     value="1.841"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="angCylLrgBkt"        value="${-pi/180*164.1}" scope="global"/>
    <!-- New suction device: shorter stand-off, marginally shorter stack. -->
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeX"     value="0.255"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToProbeZ"     value="-0.57"   scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceX" value="0.1"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurfaceZ" value="-0.262" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceX"   value="0.355"  scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distAttToContactSurfaceZ"   value="-0.832" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="fork_chain_valid"    value="true"    scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnX" value="-1.0" scope="global"/> <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE ECR88D_LongArm.m:170 -->
    <xacro:property name="distPanelTopToLiftPosnZ" value="2.0"  scope="global"/>
    <!-- IMU mounting rotations = rpy(M transpose) of the ECR88D_LongArm.m (2.1 m US unit) imu* matrices. Mounts are PER-UNIT calibration
         results: pairing a URDF from one unit with a firmware carrying another misreads arm/link by ~4 deg
         with no fault (sil/tests/test_imu_kinematics.py). -->
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_chs" value="-0.004742750 -0.008549333 -1.762994439" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_boom" value="-1.571816342 -0.566316481 -0.000590754" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_arm" value="-1.579068528 0.105440054 -0.003310372" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_link" value="-1.575935070 0.058856870 0.001290998" scope="global"/>
    <xacro:property name="imu_rpy_tilt" value="1.549803632 -0.010023128 -1.597706287" scope="global"/>
  </xacro:if>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [13] !!! NOT IN ANY SOURCE — JOINT LIMITS / EFFORT / VELOCITY !!!
       ========================================================================
       EVERY NUMBER IN THIS SECTION IS INVENTED. Read T1/T2/T3 at the top.

       Why there is nothing to copy:
         * KinematicPara_new has no min/max angle rows at all.
         * The Length Kinematic deck (13 pages, fully extracted) contains zero
           joint ranges, zero cylinder strokes, zero retracted/extended
           lengths. Its only angle, angTiltMntToTilt = +1.736 deg, is a
           mounting offset.
         * The calibration deck's only angles are procedural gates
           ("Roll = 0 deg", "Pitch > 5 deg", "Swing 180 -> 90"). The firmware
           actually uses 170 -> 80 deg (SysPar.m AngChsPnt1/2) and does not check
           roll/pitch at all. None of it bounds a joint.
       The one real limit we hold is angForkUpLimit, in section [10].

       Values below are typical-for-class figures for an 8.5 t short-swing
       excavator with a JK tiltrotator, chosen so the asset is drivable in
       Isaac without flying apart. They are NOT the ECR88's ranges.
       Written in degrees so they are obviously human-chosen, not measured.

       HOW TO REPLACE: pull the cylinder strokes, then close
       cylinder-length -> joint-angle with the pin offsets already in
       sections [4]/[5]/[6]; or digitise the CalibrationVideo/ clips.
       ======================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="limits_are_estimates"  value="true"/>

  <!-- House swing about chassis Z. Physically endless (slew ring). Modelled
       as `continuous` when this flag is true; the lo/hi below exist only so a
       revolute fallback is available for solvers that dislike continuous. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntSwingContinuous"    value="true"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntSwingLower"         value="${-pi}"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntSwingUpper"         value="${pi}"/>           <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntSwingEffort"        value="20000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) N*m -->
  <xacro:property name="jntSwingVelocity"      value="1.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) rad/s, ~9.5 rpm class figure -->

  <!-- Boom swing (offset boom about BmMnt Z). ECR88 is a boom-swing machine;
       range is asymmetric on real machines. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBoomSwingLower"     value="${-pi/180*70}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBoomSwingUpper"     value="${ pi/180*65}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBoomSwingEffort"    value="30000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBoomSwingVelocity"  value="0.35"/>            <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       OEM-TEAM JOINT ANGLES.  Source: Olivia (Xpanner, ECR88 담당), 2026-09-11.
       Quoted verbatim from the message:
           "암 : 31~155도"
           "붐 : -31(붐다운 바닥기준) ~ -69(최대)"
       These are MACHINE-convention angles, not URDF joint angles. They outrank
       every ESTIMATE below; tag = OEM-TEAM.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="oemArmAngleMin"        value="31.0"/>    <!-- OEM-TEAM [deg] -->
  <xacro:property name="oemArmAngleMax"        value="155.0"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM [deg] -->
  <xacro:property name="oemBm1AngleGround"     value="-31.0"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM [deg] "붐다운 바닥기준" -->
  <xacro:property name="oemBm1AngleMaxTravel"  value="-69.0"/>   <!-- OEM-TEAM [deg] "최대" -->
  <xacro:property name="oemBm1AngleSpan"       value="${oemBm1AngleGround - oemBm1AngleMaxTravel}"/>
                                                                 <!-- DERIVED = 38 deg total travel
                                                                      (xacro has no abs(); -31 - (-69) = +38) -->

  <!-- Boom 1 pitch.
       The OEM figures give the SPAN (38 deg) reliably, but not the zero reference,
       so the span still has to be PLACED in URDF convention. It is placed by the
       one physical statement in the message: "-31 = 붐다운 바닥기준", i.e. the
       boom-down travel stop is the posture where the tool reaches the GROUND.

       DERIVATION (scripts-free repro: expand this xacro, then sweep boom over the
       OEM arm range and bisect for the first TCP contact with the ground plane):
         ground plane, base_link frame = LenBottomZ1 = -1.445 m
         sweep contact_surface_link Z over arm in [-149, -25] deg
         first boom angle whose reachable Zmin touches the ground = +11.53 deg URDF
         => boom-down stop  = +11.53 deg  (OEM -31)
            boom-up stop    = +11.53 - 38 = -26.47 deg  (OEM -69)
            machine->URDF offset = +42.53 deg
       Sanity: in this URDF, NEGATIVE boom angle raises the tool, which is the same
       sense as the OEM numbers (-69 "최대" is the raised end). That agreement is
       what makes the placement credible rather than arbitrary.

       Before this anchor the range was placed at [-31, +7] deg and the machine
       COULD NOT REACH THE GROUND (TCP Zmin = -0.958 vs ground -1.445). That is how
       the misplacement was caught. Keep the ground-contact check when these
       numbers change.

       *** STILL ASK OLIVIA: 이 각도의 0점과 (+)방향은? 수평 기준인가 실린더 기준인가? ***
       An explicit zero reference replaces this derivation outright.
       -->
  <!-- 2026-09-11: RE-SOLVED for the 2.1 m arm. This anchor is a function of lenArm and
       of the whole tool chain below the bucket pin, so it does NOT survive a variant
       switch and must be re-bisected whenever machine_variant changes.
           arm 1.7 m  ->  +11.53 deg   (machine->URDF offset +42.53)
           arm 2.1 m  ->  -27.73 deg   (machine->URDF offset  +3.27)   THIS BUILD
       Worth noting: on the 2.1 m arm the offset between Olivia's machine frame and this
       URDF's boom frame collapses to 3.3 deg, i.e. her zero and ours very nearly coincide.
       On the 1.7 m arm it was 42.5 deg. That is weak evidence that her figures were taken
       on the 2.1 m machine (the fleet spec) rather than the 1.7 m one. Suggestive, not
       proof, since the anchor still rests on our own ground-contact assumption. It is
       another reason to ask her which arm the angles belong to. -->
  <xacro:property name="bm1GroundContactUrdf"  value="-27.73"/>
                        <!-- DERIVED [deg] boom angle at first ground contact, see above -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBm1Lower"           value="${pi/180*(bm1GroundContactUrdf - oemBm1AngleSpan)}"/>
                        <!-- = -26.47 deg, boom fully UP.   OEM-TEAM span + DERIVED placement -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBm1Upper"           value="${pi/180*bm1GroundContactUrdf}"/>
                        <!-- = +11.53 deg, boom DOWN at ground. OEM-TEAM span + DERIVED placement -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBm1Effort"          value="60000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBm1Velocity"        value="0.5"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- Boom 2. lenBm2 = 0 and cfg_dual_boom_enable = false, so this joint is
       emitted fixed. Limits kept at zero deliberately — not an estimate. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBm2Lower"           value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntBm2Upper"           value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntBm2Effort"          value="0.0"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntBm2Velocity"        value="0.0"/>

  <!-- Arm (stick).
       OEM gives the BOOM-ARM INTERIOR angle phi = 31..155 deg. The URDF arm_joint
       is zero when the arm is collinear with the boom (origin (lenBm1,0,0), no rpy),
       i.e. phi = 180 deg at theta = 0, and the arm curls in at negative theta:
           theta = -(180 - phi)
           phi = 155 (arm out)  ->  theta = -25 deg   -> upper stop
           phi =  31 (arm in)   ->  theta = -149 deg  -> lower stop
       The previous ESTIMATE was -155..-15 deg, i.e. the same convention but a
       wider span; the OEM figures narrow it. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntArmLower"           value="${-pi/180*(180 - oemArmAngleMin)}"/>
                                        <!-- = -149 deg. OEM-TEAM via interior-angle conversion -->
  <xacro:property name="jntArmUpper"           value="${-pi/180*(180 - oemArmAngleMax)}"/>
                                        <!-- =  -25 deg. OEM-TEAM via interior-angle conversion -->
  <xacro:property name="jntArmEffort"          value="40000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntArmVelocity"        value="0.6"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- Bucket / input link — drives the 4-bar. NOTE: the 4-bar itself is a
       closed chain; Isaac needs it broken with a mimic or a loop-closure
       joint. These limits apply to the INPUT link only. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntInpLinkLower"       value="${-pi/180*170}"/>  <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntInpLinkUpper"       value="${-pi/180*20}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntInpLinkEffort"      value="25000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntInpLinkVelocity"    value="0.8"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Bucket joint (= ecr88.urdf.xacro `bucket_joint`: output_link rotating
       about the bucket pin, carrying the whole tiltrotator + tool stack).
       WORSE THAN AN ESTIMATE-BY-ANALOGY: unlike every other block in this
       section there is not even a near-miss row in KinematicPara_new to
       rename — the sheet has NO bucket angle of any kind. Invented outright.
       ASSUMPTION: an 8.5 t-class bucket curl sweeps ~180 deg, biased to the
       curled side, so the tool hangs roughly vertical at zero. Taken as
       -170 deg .. +10 deg about the +Y axis the URDF asserts (T7).
       Effort/velocity are set EQUAL to the input-link block above on the
       ground that both are ends of the SAME 4-bar driven by the SAME bucket
       cylinder — that is a consistency argument, not a measurement.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktLower"           value="${-pi/180*170}"/>  <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktUpper"           value="${ pi/180*10}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktEffort"          value="25000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) same cylinder as jntInpLink -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktVelocity"        value="0.8"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) same cylinder as jntInpLink -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Connecting-rod joint (= ecr88.urdf.xacro `conn_rod_joint`). This is the
       CUT member of the 4-bar: the dressing branch, visual only, with NO
       actuator behind it. Same "no source, no renameable row" status as
       jntBkt* above.
       ASSUMPTION on the range: in the real linkage the conn-rod angle is not
       free at all — it is fully determined by the loop closure, which URDF
       cannot express. So the range here is deliberately UNCONSTRAINED
       (+/-pi) rather than a fake tight range that would fight the USD loop
       constraint once it is re-imposed. Re-impose the constraint (see the
       MIMIC / closed-loop block in ecr88.urdf.xacro) and these limits become
       irrelevant; leave it un-imposed and they are the only thing stopping
       the dressing links from spinning.
       ASSUMPTION on effort: an idler carries no drive torque, so the honest
       value is 0 — but URDF/Isaac reject a zero effort limit, hence the 1.0
       token below. Do NOT read a torque off this joint.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="jntConnRodLower"       value="${-pi}"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) unconstrained placeholder -->
  <xacro:property name="jntConnRodUpper"       value="${ pi}"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) unconstrained placeholder -->
  <xacro:property name="jntConnRodEffort"      value="1.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) token: idler, no actuator -->
  <xacro:property name="jntConnRodVelocity"    value="1.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) follows the driven branch -->

  <!-- ==================================================================
       Bucket 4-bar mimic coefficients. input_link_joint carries
       <mimic joint="bucket_joint" multiplier offset> because that is the only
       loop coupling URDF can express. The TRUE 4-bar relation is NON-LINEAR,
       so no multiplier/offset pair can be correct — 1.0 / 0.0 is chosen as
       the identity (input link tracks the output link one-for-one), which is
       the least-wrong linearisation near the mid-stroke pose and is obviously
       a placeholder rather than a fitted number.
       Fix by fitting to the real linkage, or better: drop the mimic and
       re-impose the loop as a USD/PhysX constraint.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktMimicMultiplier" value="1.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) placeholder linearisation -->
  <xacro:property name="jntBktMimicOffset"     value="0.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) placeholder linearisation -->

  <!-- Tiltrotator tilt (JK). Typical +/-45 deg on this tiltrotator class. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTiltLower"          value="${-pi/180*45}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTiltUpper"          value="${ pi/180*45}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTiltEffort"         value="8000.0"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTiltVelocity"       value="0.6"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- Tiltrotator rotate. Endless on the JK. Same continuous/revolute
       treatment as the swing. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntRotContinuous"      value="true"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntRotLower"           value="${-pi}"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntRotUpper"           value="${pi}"/>           <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntRotEffort"          value="5000.0"/>          <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntRotVelocity"        value="1.2"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- Fork tilt. The UPPER bound is real (angForkUpLimit, section [10],
       Update-after-Calibration). The lower bound is not in any source. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntForkLower"          value="${-pi/180*90}"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T1) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntForkUpper"          value="${angForkUpLimit}"/> <!-- from source -->
  <xacro:property name="jntForkEffort"         value="15000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntForkVelocity"       value="0.3"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) -->

  <!-- Travel (tracks). Not part of the kinematic sheet at all. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTrackEffort"        value="30000.0"/>         <!-- ESTIMATE (T2) -->
  <xacro:property name="jntTrackVelocity"      value="10.0"/>            <!-- ESTIMATE (T3) rad/s at the sprocket -->

  <!-- Joint damping/friction: no source whatsoever. Small non-zero values so
       Isaac's articulation solver stays stable; tune against telemetry. -->
  <xacro:property name="jnt_damping_default"   value="10.0"/>            <!-- ESTIMATE -->
  <xacro:property name="jnt_friction_default"  value="1.0"/>             <!-- ESTIMATE -->

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [14] !!! NOT IN ANY SOURCE — MASS AND INERTIA !!!
       ========================================================================
       EVERY NUMBER IN THIS SECTION IS INVENTED. Read T4/T5 at the top.

       No source we hold contains a single mass, centre of gravity or inertia
       figure. The calibration deck explicitly marks its own inertial-tensor
       calibration "(TBD)" at slide 2 row 30, so upstream has not measured it
       either.

       Method used, stated plainly so it can be audited:
         1. BOTH the total AND the per-link split are guesses. Nothing here is
            pinned to anything. The ~8.5 t figure in mass_total_reference
            below is a RECALLED brochure number, not a sourced one (T10), and
            no expression normalises the split to it.
         2. The SPLIT across links is my judgement of typical excavator mass
            distribution (roughly 45% undercarriage, 36% upper structure with
            counterweight/engine/cab, 19% front linkage + tool). It is a
            guess, link by link.
         3. Because the split is NOT normalised, the per-link values sum to
            whatever they sum to. mass_total_estimated below computes that sum
            and mass_total_split_delta computes its distance from the recalled
            8.5 t. Both exist so the discrepancy is visible and self-updating
            instead of being asserted in a comment that can go stale — which
            is exactly what the previous "pinned to 8.5 t" claim had done.
         4. Inertia tensors are NOT listed here. ecr88_inertia.xacro derives
            them with the solid-box approximation from these masses and the
            boundary/length numbers. That is a second layer of approximation
            on top of a guess — do not report simulated dynamics as
            representative of the real machine until T4/T5 are closed.

       WHICH SET IS AUTHORITATIVE: the m_* family in section [15][C], one
       entry per link ecr88.urdf.xacro actually emits. The mass_* names in
       this section are the older, parallel set; they are now DERIVED from
       m_* (see below) so the two cannot drift apart. Three of them
       (mass_boom_mount, mass_bm2, mass_fork) have no m_* counterpart because
       the current URDF emits no massive link for them — they keep their own
       ESTIMATE and are flagged as such.

       Massless-by-design (pure coordinate frames given the marker mass
       m_marker = 0.01 kg, small enough to be dynamically irrelevant and large
       enough that neither the URDF importer nor PhysX trips over it):
       boom_mount, the three cyl_* anchors, ant_main, ant_aux.
       ======================================================================== -->
  <xacro:property name="masses_are_estimates"  value="true"/>
  <!-- ESTIMATE (T10). NOT a published figure we hold: this is a recalled Volvo
       ECR88(D) brochure operating weight, and it is NOT in any document in
       resources/ (no operating-weight row exists in any of the three extracts).
       It is a sanity reference ONLY — nothing is normalised to it. Confirm it
       against the real ECR88(D) spec sheet, or delete it. -->
  <!-- mass_total_reference: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->  <!-- ESTIMATE (T10) recalled brochure figure, unsourced -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [14][C] AUTHORITATIVE MASS SET — m_*, one entry per link that
       ecr88.urdf.xacro actually emits. This is the set the URDF consumes
       (its "REQUIRED PROPERTY CONTRACT" block, section [C]).
       EVERY value is ESTIMATE (T4). Kilograms.
       .................................................................. -->
  <!-- m_base: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->  <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) track frame / centre car body, tracks excluded -->
  <!-- m_track: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) PER TRACK — emitted twice (left + right) -->
  <!-- m_house: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->  <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) upper structure + counterweight + engine + cab -->
  <!-- m_boom: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 3.55 m boom (lenBm1) -->
  <!-- m_arm: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 1.7 m arm; scale ~x1.2 for the 2.1 m variant -->
  <!-- m_gnd_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->     <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 4-bar ground link (arm-side member, 0.2597 m) -->
  <!-- m_inp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 4-bar input/crank link -->
  <!-- m_conn_rod: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 4-bar connecting rod (idler, cut member) -->
  <!-- m_outp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) 4-bar output link — the load path to the tool -->
  <!-- m_tilt_mount: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) -->
  <!-- m_tilt: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) JK tilt body -->
  <!-- m_rotator: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) JK rotator body -->
  <!-- m_attachment: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) suction adapter -->
  <!-- m_probe: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) probe stand-off member -->
  <!-- m_contact_surface: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) suction plate assembly (the TCP link) -->
  <!-- Marker/frame links (boom_mount, gnss_main, gnss_aux, the three cyl_*
       anchors). Not massless — see the note above. -->
  <!-- m_marker: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) coordinate-frame markers, 6 of them -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       Total of the split, COMPUTED — never hand-typed, so it cannot go stale
       the way the old "pinned to 8.5 t" comment did. Tracks counted twice,
       markers six times (the six frame links listed above).
       .................................................................. -->
  <!-- mass_total_estimated: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->
  <!-- How far the guessed split lands from the recalled 8.5 t (T10). Positive
       = the split is heavier. This is NOT an error term to be driven to zero:
       both sides are guesses. It is here so a reader sees the size of the
       uncertainty instead of a false claim of agreement. -->
  <!-- mass_total_split_delta: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       LEGACY mass_* NAMES — DERIVED from m_* above, not independent numbers.
       They exist because ecr88_inertia.xacro and the earlier draft of this
       file used this vocabulary. Nothing in ecr88.urdf.xacro reads them.
       Do NOT put a literal back into any of the aliased rows: that is how the
       two disagreeing mass sets came about in the first place.
       .................................................................. -->
  <!-- mass_undercarriage: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->  <!-- alias: base + both tracks -->
  <!-- mass_house: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->      <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_bm1: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->       <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_bm2: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->     <!-- lenBm2 = 0, segment absent. Not an estimate. -->
  <!-- mass_arm: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->        <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_inp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_conn_rod: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->   <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_outp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->  <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_tilt_mnt: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro --> <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_tilt: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->        <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_rot: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->    <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_att: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro --> <!-- alias -->
  <!-- mass_frame_only: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->     <!-- alias -->
  <!-- No m_* counterpart: the current URDF emits boom_mount_link as a marker
       frame and emits no fork link at all (the fork is the PanelLift variant,
       section [10]). These two stay independent ESTIMATEs and are NOT part of
       mass_total_estimated, because no link carries them today. -->
  <xacro:property name="mass_boom_mount"       value="80.0"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) boom-swing bracket, not emitted as a massive link -->
  <xacro:property name="mass_fork"             value="180.0"/>   <!-- ESTIMATE (T4) PanelLift fork, not emitted by this URDF -->

  <!-- Payload carried by the suction plate. Not a machine property — a
       scenario property — but it dominates the front-end dynamics, so it is
       parameterised here rather than buried in a scene file. Default 0. -->
  <xacro:property name="mass_panel_payload"    value="0.0"/>     <!-- set per scenario -->

  <!-- Inertia derivation control, consumed by ecr88_inertia.xacro.
       'box'      : solid cuboid from the link's primitive extents (default)
       'cylinder' : solid cylinder about its long axis (booms/arm)
       'from_mesh': requires use_meshes = true AND real densities. Not usable
                    yet — no meshes, no densities. -->
  <xacro:property name="inertia_model"         value="box"/>
  <!-- Structural steel weldments are hollow. A solid-box tensor overestimates
       off-axis inertia badly, so the derived tensor is scaled by this factor.
       0.6 is a rule of thumb for fabricated box-section booms. ESTIMATE (T5). -->
  <xacro:property name="inertia_hollow_factor" value="0.6"/>
  <!-- Floor on the diagonal so Isaac never sees a singular tensor on the
       zero-mass frame links. -->
  <xacro:property name="inertia_min_diag"      value="1e-6"/>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       [15] URDF PROPERTY CONTRACT
       ========================================================================
       ecr88.urdf.xacro declares a "REQUIRED PROPERTY CONTRACT" at its head and
       reads NOTHING else from this file. Until this section existed the two
       files were written against different vocabularies and had never been
       expanded together.

       Everything below is one of exactly two things, and the comment on each
       row says which:
         ALIAS     — a second name for a property defined above. No new number
                     enters the file. If you need to change the value, change
                     it at the definition, never here.
         ESTIMATE  — a value that exists in NO source we hold, authored here
                     because the URDF cannot expand without it. Every one
                     carries its TODO tag and its assumption.

       Placed LAST on purpose: the variant blocks in section [12] define
       lenArm / distArmToInpLinkX / distAttToProbe* / distProbeToContactSurface*
       inside <xacro:if>, so an alias to them is only safe after [12] has run.
       Do not move this section up the file.
       ======================================================================== -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][A] Frame offsets, per axis.  ALL ALIASES — section [4]/[7]/[12].
       The URDF wants dist<Name>_x/_y/_z; the sheet spells them dist<Name>X/Y/Z.
       .................................................................. -->
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMnt_x"      value="${distChsToBmMntX}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMnt_y"      value="${distChsToBmMntY}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distChsToBmMnt_z"      value="${distChsToBmMntZ}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1_x"      value="${distBmMntToBm1X}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1_y"      value="${distBmMntToBm1Y}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distBmMntToBm1_z"      value="${distBmMntToBm1Z}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLink_x"    value="${distArmToInpLinkX}"/>            <!-- ALIAS (variant-dependent, [12]) -->
  <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLink_y"    value="${distArmToInpLinkY}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distArmToInpLink_z"    value="${distArmToInpLinkZ}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMnt_x" value="${distOutpLinkToTiltMntX}"/>     <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMnt_y" value="${distOutpLinkToTiltMntY}"/>     <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distOutpLinkToTiltMnt_z" value="${distOutpLinkToTiltMntZ}"/>     <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTilt_x"   value="${distTiltMntToTiltX}"/>           <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTilt_y"   value="${distTiltMntToTiltY}"/>           <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distTiltMntToTilt_z"   value="${distTiltMntToTiltZ}"/>           <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distRotToAtt_x"        value="${distRotToAttX}"/>                <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distRotToAtt_y"        value="${distRotToAttY}"/>                <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distRotToAtt_z"        value="${distRotToAttZ}"/>                <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distAttToProbe_x"      value="${distAttToProbeX}"/>              <!-- ALIAS (variant-dependent, [12]) -->
  <xacro:property name="distAttToProbe_y"      value="${distAttToProbeY}"/>              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distAttToProbe_z"      value="${distAttToProbeZ}"/>              <!-- ALIAS (variant-dependent, [12]) -->

  <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurface_x" value="${distProbeToContactSurfaceX}"/> <!-- ALIAS (variant-dependent, [12]) -->
  <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurface_y" value="${distProbeToContactSurfaceY}"/> <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distProbeToContactSurface_z" value="${distProbeToContactSurfaceZ}"/> <!-- ALIAS (variant-dependent, [12]) -->

  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChs_x"    value="${distAntMainToChsX}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChs_y"    value="${distAntMainToChsY}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToChs_z"    value="${distAntMainToChsZ}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAux_x" value="${distAntMainToAntAuxX}"/>         <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAux_y" value="${distAntMainToAntAuxY}"/>         <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distAntMainToAntAux_z" value="${distAntMainToAntAuxZ}"/>         <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1_x"       value="${distCylLrgBm1X}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1_y"       value="${distCylLrgBm1Y}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgBm1_z"       value="${distCylLrgBm1Z}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1_x"       value="${distCylSmlBm1X}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1_y"       value="${distCylSmlBm1Y}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylSmlBm1_z"       value="${distCylSmlBm1Z}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArm_x"       value="${distCylLrgArmX}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArm_y"       value="${distCylLrgArmY}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="distCylLrgArm_z"       value="${distCylLrgArmZ}"/>               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][B] Boundary boxes.  ALL ALIASES — section [9].
       The source spells the SIZES LenUpX / LenUpY but the ORIGINS LenUppX1 /
       LenUppY1 / LenUppZ1 (its own inconsistency, preserved above). The URDF
       normalises everything to lenUpp*, hence the two odd-looking rows.
       .................................................................. -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBottomX1"           value="${LenBottomX1}"/>                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBottomY1"           value="${LenBottomY1}"/>                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBottomZ1"           value="${LenBottomZ1}"/>                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBottomX"            value="${LenBottomX}"/>                   <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenBottomY"            value="${LenBottomY}"/>                   <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenUppX1"              value="${LenUppX1}"/>                     <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenUppY1"              value="${LenUppY1}"/>                     <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenUppZ1"              value="${LenUppZ1}"/>                     <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="lenUppX"               value="${LenUpX}"/>                       <!-- ALIAS of LenUpX (source spelling) -->
  <xacro:property name="lenUppY"               value="${LenUpY}"/>                       <!-- ALIAS of LenUpY (source spelling) -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][C] Masses — defined in section [14] (m_* block). Nothing here.
       .................................................................. -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][D] Primitive placeholder geometry.  ESTIMATE (T11) unless marked
       ALIAS. These are cross-section dimensions for the boxes/cylinders drawn
       while use_meshes = false. No source we hold contains any of them, and
       they also drive the inertia tensors (T5), so a wrong radius is a wrong
       tensor, not just a wrong picture.
       .................................................................. -->
  <!-- Track shoe width. ASSUMPTION: 0.45 m, chosen so the two track boxes plus
       the centre car body exactly fill the measured undercarriage width
       lenBottomY = 2.32 (leaving a 1.42 m centre frame, which is plausible for
       this class). The 0.45 itself is not sourced. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_track_w"           value="0.45"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m -->
  <!-- House height. NOT a fresh guess: reuses the T6 derivation in section [9]
       (cab roof one bolt-height below the antenna plane), so the house box and
       the boundary-box height can never disagree. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_house_h"           value="${LenUpZ_est}"/>                   <!-- ALIAS of the T6 derivation -->
  <!-- Structural member radii. ASSUMPTION: fabricated box-section members
       approximated by a circumscribed-ish cylinder — boom heavier than arm,
       both far heavier than the 4-bar links. Nothing sourced. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_r"            value="0.20"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m, 3.55 m boom -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_arm_r"             value="0.15"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m, 1.7 m arm -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_linkage_r"         value="0.05"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m, 4-bar links + probe member -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_tr_r"              value="0.18"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m, JK tiltrotator body -->
  <!-- Suction pad footprint. NOT a fresh guess: reuses the T9 stand-in from
       section [11] (pad sized to the panel it lifts) because the source
       records LenSDX = LenSDY = 0 for ECR88. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_pad_x"             value="${LenSDX_est}"/>                   <!-- ALIAS of the T9 stand-in -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_pad_y"             value="${LenSDY_est}"/>                   <!-- ALIAS of the T9 stand-in -->
  <!-- Pad plate thickness. No source at any level — LenSDZ (-0.84) is the whole
       adapter stack height, not the plate. ASSUMPTION: 60 mm plate. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_pad_t"             value="0.06"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m -->
  <!-- Cosmetic only: radius of the marker spheres drawn at the pure coordinate
       frames (gnss, cyl anchors, boom mount). Not machine data. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_marker_r"          value="0.03"/>    <!-- ESTIMATE (T11) m, cosmetic -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][D3] FORK CARRIAGE, PANEL STACK, SUCTION CUPS.
       Shape for the part of the machine that actually feeds it: modules ride on a
       forklift carriage at the front and the tool lifts them off one at a time.
       Positions come from the sheet (LenPLX/Y/Z, AlphaPL1/2, LenPLWidth,
       LenPanelHeight); the sizes below are the bits the sheet does not carry.
       .................................................................. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_fork_clear"        value="0.15"/>   <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, carriage root ahead of the track front -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_fork_len"          value="1.06"/>    <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  m, lenForkBlade. Same 1.06 in BOTH ECR88D_LongArm.m and ECR88D_ShortArm.m, so it is the carriage, not the arm variant. Our 1.45 ASSUMPTION was 37 % long. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_fork_t"            value="0.07"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, pallet/backrest plate -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_fork_back_h"       value="1.25"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, backrest height -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_panel_t"           value="0.030"/>   <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  m, panelThickness. Only ECR88D_ShortArm.m carries it; the long arm file has no panel block, so this is the Korean machine's module and the US one may differ. -->
  <xacro:property name="n_panels"              value="12"/>      <!-- scene choice, not data. A FULL stack is panels_per_stack below; 12 is what we draw so the fork is not hidden behind glass. -->
  <xacro:property name="panels_per_stack"      value="30"/>      <!-- OEM-FIRMWARE  panelCntPerStack, ECR88D_ShortArm.m. 30 x 30 mm = 0.90 m of glass, which is the height the fork and the pick sequence are designed around. -->
  <xacro:property name="m_panel"               value="23.0"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  kg, 72-cell glass-glass -->
  <xacro:property name="m_fork"                value="280.0"/>   <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  kg, carriage + backrest -->
  <!-- Suction cup. The sheet records LenSDX = LenSDY = 0 for ECR88, i.e. the pad was
       never measured, so the cups are sized to what lifts a module: four 200 mm cups
       is the usual arrangement for a ~25 kg glass panel. CLASS-TYPICAL. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_suction_cup_r"     value="0.10"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  m -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_suction_cup_h"     value="0.06"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  m -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][D2] SHAPE, not size.  Added 2026-09-11 after the first Isaac Sim
       view: the kinematics were right but the machine did not read as an
       excavator, because every member was a straight rod and the whole upper
       structure was one featureless 2 m cube.

       None of this changes a joint origin, a link length, a mass or an inertia.
       It only changes what the placeholder primitives LOOK like, and it is worth
       doing because the entire point of milestone 1 is letting people judge
       sensor placement by eye. A cube tells them nothing about where a camera
       would actually see from.

       The section dimensions below are PUBLISHED: Volvo "Boom and dipper arm"
       dimension table (the sheet Teo shared 2026-09-11).
           Mono boom 3.55 m : length 3700, height 1244, width  335 mm
           Dipper 1.7 m     : length 2283, height  518, width  305 mm
           Dipper 2.1 m     : length 2684, height  562, width  305 mm
       Note LENGTH here is the physical part envelope (3700), which is longer than
       lenBm1 = 3550, the PIN-TO-PIN distance the kinematics use. They are different
       quantities and both are correct; do not "fix" one against the other.
       .................................................................. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_sec_h"        value="0.335"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED  boom section width 335 mm, used as the beam depth -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_sec_w"        value="0.335"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED  boom width 335 mm -->
  <!-- The published 1244 mm boom envelope height is what the BEND costs: a straight
       3.55 m member would envelope at roughly its own section depth. So the rise of
       the arc above the pin-to-pin chord is the envelope height minus the section:
       1.244 - 0.335 = 0.909 m. ASSUMPTION: apex at 45% of the chord, which is where
       it sits on the Volvo side-view drawing. Both numbers are cosmetic. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_h_env"        value="1.244"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED  boom envelope height 1244 mm -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_rise"         value="${geo_boom_h_env - geo_boom_sec_h}"/>
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_apex_frac"    value="0.45"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  cosmetic -->
  <!-- Dipper section, per variant. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_arm_sec_h"         value="${0.562 if lenArm &gt; 1.9 else 0.518}"/> <!-- PUBLISHED -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_arm_sec_w"         value="0.305"/>   <!-- PUBLISHED  both dipper lengths are 305 mm wide -->
  <!-- Upper structure broken into the three masses a person actually recognises.
       Heights split geo_house_h; footprint split lenUppX / lenUppY. All ASSUMPTION,
       all cosmetic, none of it feeds inertia (m_house keeps its single box). -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_deck_h"            value="${geo_house_h * 0.46}"/>  <!-- ASSUMPTION  engine deck height -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cab_x"             value="1.00"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  cab footprint, m -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cab_y"             value="0.92"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION -->
  <!-- Cab height is the REMAINDER of geo_house_h above the deck, not an independent
       guess. That keeps the three solids inside the same overall envelope the single
       box used, so the machine does not silently grow taller than the T6 derivation
       says it is: deck + cab = geo_house_h exactly. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cab_h"             value="${geo_house_h - geo_deck_h}"/>  <!-- DERIVED from geo_house_h -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cw_x"              value="0.62"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  counterweight depth, m -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cw_h"              value="${geo_house_h * 0.62}"/>  <!-- ASSUMPTION -->
  <!-- Dozer blade. The machine has one: the calibration procedure gates on
       "Dozer full down (Pitch angle > 5)". Cosmetic here, but it is the shape that
       most says "compact excavator" at a glance. Drawn on base_link, not articulated. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_blade_h"           value="0.42"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, blade plate height -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_blade_t"           value="0.13"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  m, blade plate thickness -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_blade_arm"         value="1.10"/>    <!-- CLASS-TYPICAL  m, pivot on the track frame to the cutting edge -->
  <!-- Blade pivot, on the track frame just ahead of the front idler and near the track
       bottom, which is where a compact excavator carries it. ASSUMPTION. -->
  <xacro:property name="blade_pivot_x"         value="${lenBottomX * 0.5 - 0.10}"/>
  <xacro:property name="blade_pivot_z"         value="${lenBottomZ1 + 0.22}"/>

  <!-- BLADE RANGE. The firmware publishes JntAngDozerBlade and lists autoCtrlEnaDozer,
       so this is a commanded axis, but neither the sheet nor the firmware carries its
       limits. There IS a hard requirement to derive a floor from, though: the
       calibration procedure will not start unless the blade can hold "Dozer full down,
       Pitch angle > 5 deg", i.e. the blade alone has to jack the front of the machine
       off the ground by 5 deg.
         rear idler to cutting edge    = lenBottomX/2 + 0.10 + geo_blade_arm*cos(down)
                                       ~ 2.62 m at the angle of interest
         drop needed for 5 deg of pitch = 2.62 * tan(5 deg) = 0.229 m below the tracks
         with geo_blade_arm = 1.10 m    = asin(0.229/1.10) = 12.0 deg below horizontal
       So ANY range narrower than 12 deg down is provably wrong: it would fail a gate
       the real machine passes. We take roughly double that, which also lands on the
       class norm (blades in this size drop and lift about 0.4 m):
         down 25 deg = 0.465 m below the tracks
         up   30 deg = 0.550 m above
       Tag CLASS-TYPICAL with a DERIVED floor. Replace from the working-range drawing
       Teo has; that drawing carries the real numbers. -->
  <xacro:property name="jntDozerLower"         value="${-pi/180*25}"/>
  <xacro:property name="jntDozerUpper"         value="${ pi/180*30}"/>

  <!-- Crawler side profile. A track is a belt wrapped round an idler and a sprocket,
       so its silhouette is a rounded rectangle, not a slab. Rolling radius is half the
       track height (lenUppZ1 - lenBottomZ1, the measured car-body clearance), and the
       two wheels sit one radius in from each end so the belt envelope still spans
       exactly lenBottomX. Derived from measured numbers, so the track can never
       outgrow the surveyed undercarriage; only the roundness is cosmetic. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_track_h"           value="${lenUppZ1 - lenBottomZ1}"/>   <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_track_r"           value="${geo_track_h * half}"/>       <!-- DERIVED -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_track_wheel_dx"    value="${lenBottomX * half - geo_track_r}"/> <!-- DERIVED -->
  <!-- Cab glasshouse: the upper box is inset so the cab reads as a cab rather than a
       second counterweight. Cosmetic. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_cab_upper_inset"   value="0.14"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION  m -->
  <!-- Boom taper: the tip section is narrower than the foot section on every excavator
       boom. Fraction of geo_boom_sec_*. Cosmetic. -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_boom_taper"        value="0.72"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION -->
  <xacro:property name="geo_arm_taper"         value="0.68"/>    <!-- ASSUMPTION -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][E] Joint limits / actuation.  ALL ALIASES onto section [13].
       No number is authored here — section [13] holds every value together
       with its T1/T2/T3 assumption, including the jntBkt* and jntConnRod*
       blocks that had no counterpart in the sheet at all.
       Joint-name map (URDF joint  <-  sheet-vocabulary block):
         swing_joint     <- jntSwing       boom_joint      <- jntBm1
         arm_joint       <- jntArm         bucket_joint    <- jntBkt
         input_link_joint<- jntInpLink     conn_rod_joint  <- jntConnRod
         tilt_joint      <- jntTilt        rotator_joint   <- jntRot
       rotator_joint is `continuous` in the URDF, so it takes effort/velocity
       only — jntRotLower/Upper stay unaliased on purpose (they are the
       revolute-fallback pair described in section [13]).
       .................................................................. -->
  <!-- lim_swing_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_swing_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_swing: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->               <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_swing: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->             <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_boom_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_boom_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_boom: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_boom: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_arm_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_arm_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_arm: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_arm: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_bucket_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_bucket_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                  <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_bucket: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_bucket: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->               <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_inp_link_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_inp_link_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_inp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->             <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_inp_link: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->           <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_conn_rod_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_conn_rod_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->              <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_conn_rod: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->             <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_conn_rod: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->           <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- lim_tilt_lower: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- lim_tilt_upper: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- eff_tilt: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- vel_tilt: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->              <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- eff_rotator: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->                 <!-- ALIAS (continuous: no lim_*) -->
  <!-- vel_rotator: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->               <!-- ALIAS (continuous: no lim_*) -->

  <!-- dyn_damping: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->          <!-- ALIAS -->
  <!-- dyn_friction: now defined in ecr88_dynamics.xacro -->         <!-- ALIAS -->

  <!-- ..................................................................
       [15][F] Bucket 4-bar mimic coefficients.  ALIASES onto section [13].
       .................................................................. -->
  <xacro:property name="bkt_mimic_mult"        value="${jntBktMimicMultiplier}"/>        <!-- ALIAS -->
  <xacro:property name="bkt_mimic_off"         value="${jntBktMimicOffset}"/>            <!-- ALIAS -->

</robot>
